+{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"call_04_h5XKlujjJYmck1sP7Zlt2839","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Task Queue System\n2\t\n3\tBuild a fully-featured in-memory task queue system from scratch. The system should grow incrementally through 12 phases, each introducing new capabilities that build on the foundation laid by earlier phases.\n4\t\n5\t## Overview\n6\t\n7\tYour task queue should support adding tasks, processing them, and managing their lifecycle. Think of it like a simplified version of systems like Bull, BeeQueue, or Celery -- but entirely in-memory with no external dependencies.\n8\t\n9\tThe system evolves through 12 phases of increasing sophistication:\n10\t\n11\t1. **Basic queue** -- adding and processing tasks in order\n12\t2. **Named queues** -- multiple independent queues\n13\t3. **Priority** -- some tasks are more urgent than others\n14\t4. **Delayed tasks** -- tasks that should wait before becoming available\n15\t5. **Retry with backoff** -- automatic retry when tasks fail\n16\t6. **Dead letter queue** -- permanent storage for repeatedly-failing tasks\n17\t7. **Task dependencies** -- tasks that wait for other tasks to finish\n18\t8. **Concurrency control** -- limiting how many tasks run at once\n19\t9. **Progress and cancellation** -- tracking and aborting work\n20\t10. **Recurring tasks** -- cron-like scheduled task creation\n21\t11. **Middleware** -- intercepting and transforming tasks in a pipeline\n22\t12. **Graceful shutdown** -- orderly wind-down of the system\n23\t\n24\tRead `phases/*.md` for detailed requirements for each phase.\n25\t\n26\t## Getting Started\n27\t\n28\t- Export your main API from `src/index.ts`\n29\t- All implementation should live under `src/`\n30\t- No external runtime dependencies allowed (dev dependencies are pre-installed)\n31\t- Write your own tests as you go to verify your implementation\n32\t\n33\t## Validation\n34\t\n35\t```bash\n36\tnpm run build   # TypeScript must compile\n37\tnpm run lint    # No lint errors\n38\t```\n39\t\n40\t## Tips\n41\t\n42\t- Later phases will force you to rethink early decisions. Design with extensibility in mind.\n43\t- Tasks should have unique identifiers and track their lifecycle status.\n44\t- Consider how features like priorities, delays, and dependencies interact with each other.\n45\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2dc7e0af-5fc2-4b88-b1fc-844f6d81dc8e","uuid":"21d816f2-c4f3-4b10-bda0-3c9362a3265d","timestamp":"2026-05-31T03:53:04.849Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/TASK.md","content":"# Task Queue System\n\nBuild a fully-featured in-memory task queue system from scratch. The system should grow incrementally through 12 phases, each introducing new capabilities that build on the foundation laid by earlier phases.\n\n## Overview\n\nYour task queue should support adding tasks, processing them, and managing their lifecycle. Think of it like a simplified version of systems like Bull, BeeQueue, or Celery -- but entirely in-memory with no external dependencies.\n\nThe system evolves through 12 phases of increasing sophistication:\n\n1. **Basic queue** -- adding and processing tasks in order\n2. **Named queues** -- multiple independent queues\n3. **Priority** -- some tasks are more urgent than others\n4. **Delayed tasks** -- tasks that should wait before becoming available\n5. **Retry with backoff** -- automatic retry when tasks fail\n6. **Dead letter queue** -- permanent storage for repeatedly-failing tasks\n7. **Task dependencies** -- tasks that wait for other tasks to finish\n8. **Concurrency control** -- limiting how many tasks run at once\n9. **Progress and cancellation** -- tracking and aborting work\n10. **Recurring tasks** -- cron-like scheduled task creation\n11. **Middleware** -- intercepting and transforming tasks in a pipeline\n12. **Graceful shutdown** -- orderly wind-down of the system\n\nRead `phases/*.md` for detailed requirements for each phase.\n\n## 