onst task = system.getNext()\nif (task) {\n  await system.process(task.id)  // runs middleware chain then handler\n}\n```\n\nOr maybe `process()` is always called behind the scenes when `getNext()` returns a task and the handler is registered? No, that doesn't make sense because the handler is separate.\n\nLet me just implement it as a `processTask(taskId)` method that:\n1. Finds the task (must be in 'processing' state)\n2. Gets the queue's middleware chain and handler\n3. If no handler is registered, it's a no-op\n4. Runs each middleware function in order (each calls `next()`)\n5. Runs the handler function\n6. If any middleware or the handler throws, mark task as failed\n\nThis seems like the most natural interpretation. Let me implement it this way.\n\nNow let me actually start implementing. I'll write all the files.\n\nOne more thing - the cron support for Phase 10. The requirements say:\n- `*/N * * * *` -- every N minutes\n- `0 * * * *` -- every hour on the hour\n- `0 0 * * *` -- every day at midnight\n\nI need to parse these simple cron expressions and determine if a schedule is due. A manual tick() method evaluates all schedules and enqueues tasks for any that are due.\n\nCron format: `minute hour day-of-month month day-of-week`\n- `*` = any\n- `*/N` = every N (within that field)\n\nFor simplicity, I'll parse the cron expression and check if the current time matches.\n\nThe tick mechanism:\n1. Get current time\n2. For each schedule, check if it's due (hasn't been run within the matching period)\n3. If due, enqueue a task and update lastRun\n\nLet me implement this now. I'll write everything in a comprehensive way.\n\nLet me think about the file structure:\n\n```\nsrc/\n  types.ts       - All type definitions\n  task.ts        - Task creation and helpers\n  queue.ts       - Internal queue management (per-queue state)\n  scheduler.ts   - Cron schedule management\n  system.ts      - Main TaskQueueSystem class (or index.ts)\n  index.ts       - Re-exports\n```\n\nA