sk (as I do now)\n2. Follow dependencies from the dependency to find the new task\n\nActually, what about this scenario:\n```javascript\nconst a = q.add('a', { dependsOn: ['self'] });\n```\n\nNo, this doesn't work either because the task doesn't have an ID at the time the dependsOn is specified.\n\nOK let me just fix the tests and also put in a more robust check. For the tests, I'll test self-reference detection and also the scenario where we'd need to create tasks in reverse order.\n\nLet me think of this differently. Instead of checking `wouldCreateCycle` by following dependents, I should follow DEPENDENCIES:\n\n```javascript\nfunction wouldCreateCycle(dependentId, dependsOnId, taskMap) {\n  // Does dependsOnId transitively depend on dependentId?\n  // Start from dependsOnId, follow its dependencies.\n  // If one of them is dependentId (directly or transitively), there's a cycle.\n}\n```\n\nWait, but the dependencies of `dependsOnId` are fixed. They were set when the task was created. If I start from `dependsOnId` and follow its dependencies, and one of them is `dependentId`, that would be: dependentId depends on dependsOnId AND dependsOnId depends on dependentId. This is a cycle!\n\nExample:\n1. `q.add('a')` → A has no deps\n2. `q.add('b', { dependsOn: ['a'] })` → B depends on A. Check: does A depend on B? A has no deps → no cycle.\n3. `q.add('c', { dependsOn: ['b'] })` → C depends on B. Check: does B depend on C? B depends on A → no.\n\nBut what about:\n1. `q.add('x')` → X has no deps\n2. `q.add('y', { dependsOn: ['x'] })` → Y depends on X. Check: does X depend on Y? No.\n3. Now, same step 2 but with Y depending on both X and something that will be created later? No, we can't do that.\n\nHmm, I still don't see how a cycle can be created with sequential creation. Unless:\n\n```javascript\nconst a = q.add('a', { dependsOn: ['b'] });\n// Wait, b doesn't exist yet! This throws.\n```\n\nSo we can't even create a depending on something that hasn't been created yet. The API forces a topological order.\n\nI think the cleanest approach is:\n1. Fix my cycle detection to also check by following the DEPENDENCIES (not just dependents) of the dependency, to see if it reaches the new task\n2. Also add a self-reference check\n3. Fix the tests\n\nLet me update the code and tests.\n\nFor the `wouldCreateCycle` function, I'll change it to check both directions:\n\n```javascript\nfunction wouldCreateCycle(taskId, dependsOnIds, dependentsMap, taskQueueMap, queues) {\n  // Direction 1: Does any dependency (transitively) depend ON the new task?\n  // Follow dependents from the dependency\n  // ... existing logic ...\n  \n  // Direction 2: Can we reach the task from any dependency by following dependencies?\n  // This catches cases where the new task's dependency transitively depends on the new task.\n  for (const depId of dependsOnIds) {\n    if (wouldReachFollowingDeps(taskId, depId, ...)) return true;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nActually wait, Direction 2: If task depends on depId, and depId transitively depends on task -> that means task depends on depId AND depId depends on task -> CYCLE. But depId was created before task, so depId can't depend on task because task didn't exist yet!\n\nOK so Direction 2 can never be true either. Unless... the task ID is predictable, but we use UUIDs so it's not.\n\nI'm going in circles (pun intended). Let me just check:\n\nIn a system where:\n- Tasks are created one at a time\n- Dependencies can only reference existing task IDs\n- Existing tasks can't be modified\n\nA circular dependency is impossible. Period.\n\nBut the spec says to detect them. So let me implement the check anyway, even if it's technically impossible with the API design. I'll check both directions for completeness, and have a self-reference check too.\n\nActually, here's the scenario for a self-reference that would work:\n```javascript\nconst a = q.add('a');\n// We can't make a depend on itself at creation time.\n// But we could create b depending on a, and then create c depending on b,\n// and c also registers as a dependency of a... we can't because a already exists.\n```\n\nI'll just fix the tests. For the circular dependency test, I'll use a scenario where:\n1. Two tasks are created that reference each other's NOT-YET-KNOWN-IDs. Since they don't know each other's IDs, this can't happen.\n\nOK, I'll just rewrite the circular dependency tests to check that a self-referencing chain is caught at some level. Actually, let me just leave the detection code in place but fix the tests to test what actually works.\n\nHere's what CAN be detected:\n1. A task depending on itself (but the task doesn't know its own ID at creation time, so this can't be tested unless the user passes the ID, which they don't have)\n2. Through the dependents map: if a new task depends on an existing task, and that existing task transitively depends on the new task. But since the new task doesn't exist yet, no one can depend on it.\n\nSo actually, in our system, circular dependencies are IMPOSSIBLE. The tests are fundamentally wrong. Let me just update the tests to be no-ops or test something else about dependencies.\n\nAlternatively, I can restructure the code so that when we add a task that depends on another, we ALSO check if the other's dependencies include our new task's \"would-be\" ancestors. But since the new task isn't in the system yet, this is impossible.\n\nI think the most reasonable approach is to change the tests to test for:\n1. Self-referencing at creation time (even though it can't happen with random UUIDs, check if someone tries to add a task that depends on its own ID)\n2. A simpler cycle detection test using a mock\n\nLet me just fix the tests and move on. I'll also improve the cycle detection to handle more scenarios.\n\nFor now, let me fix the actual code bugs first and fix the test issues:\n\n1. Fix the `fail()` method to use `<=` instead of `<`\n2. Fix the backoff test tolerance\n3. Fix the test that calls `complete` on a task not in activeTasks\n4. Fix circular dependency tests to test actual scenarios\n5. Fix the test for \"deps already completed\" - this might be a concurrency issue\n6. Fix the \"getNext returns null\" after completing dep\n7. Fix middleware error handling\n\nLet me look at all the issues more carefully:\n\n### Fix 1: Backoff calculation\n\nThe test gets backoff of 4010 instead of 4000. The issue is that `task.availableAt` is set to `Date.now() + delay` in the `fail()` method. The test then reads `task.availableAt!.getTime() - Date.now()`. There's a race condition where some time has passed between when `availableAt` was set and when the difference is computed.\n\nThe tolerance of 100 should be enough, but the actual difference is 4010ms. That means about 10ms passed between the set and the read. That's normal. Let me just increase the tolerance to 200ms.\n\nActually wait, the error says `expected 4010 to be less than 100`. The check is `Math.abs(t3Delay - 4000) < 100`. The actual t3Delay is 4010 (4000ms backoff + 10ms elapsed). `4010 - 4000 = 10 < 100` should be true! \n\nOh wait, `t3Delay` is `task.availableAt!.getTime() - Date.now()`. If `availableAt` was set to `Date.now() + 4000`, and then `Date.now() - (Date.now() + 4000)` = -4000 approximately. So the absolute value is 4000. But 10ms later, `t3Delay = (previous.now.plus.4000) - later.now = 4000 - 10 = 3990`. So `Math.abs(3990 - 4000) = 10 < 100`. This should pass.\n\nBut the error says `expected 4010 to be less than 100`! That means `Math.abs(t3Delay - 4000) = 4010`. So `Math.abs(t3Delay) = 8010` or `t3Delay = -10` and `Math.abs(-10 - 4000) = 4010`.\n\nWait, actually the backoff calculation is: `delay = task.backoffDelay * Math.pow(2, task.attempts - 1)`.\n\nOn the third fail, `task.attempts` is 3: `10 * Math.pow(2, 3-1) = 10 * 4 = 40ms`. Wait, `backoffDelay` is 10ms. `10 * 2^(3-1) = 10 * 4 = 40ms`. So the delay should be 40ms, not 4000ms!\n\nBut the test says `expect(Math.abs(t3Delay - 4000)).toBeLessThan(100)`. The 4000 is for `backoffDelay: 1000ms` in the test:\n\n```javascript\nit('should calculate exponential backoff correctly', () => {\n    const task = q.add('job', { backoffDelay: 1000 });\n    // ...\n    // Attempt 3: 1000 * 2^2 = 4000ms delay\n```\n\nAh, `backoffDelay: 1000`. 1000 * 2^2 = 4000. And the actual delay reported is 4010 (4000 + 10ms elapsed). So `Math.abs(4010 - 4000) = 10`. That should pass!\n\nWait, the error message says `expected 4010 to be less than 100`. So `Math.abs(t3Delay - 4000) = 4010`. That means `t3Delay - 4000 = 4010` or `t3Delay - 4000 = -4010`.\n\nIf `t3Delay = 8010`: `availableAt - Date.now() = 8010`. But we set it to `Date.now() + 4000`. So 4010 more than expected. That means 4010ms passed between our set and the read, which is impossible for a test that takes a few ms.\n\nUnless... the test has a bug. Let me trace through:\n\n```javascript\nconst task = q.add('job', { backoffDelay: 1000 });\n\n// Attempt 1: 1000 * 2^0 = 1000ms delay\nq.getNext();\nq.fail(task.id);\nconst t1Delay = task.availableAt!.getTime() - Date.now();\nexpect(Math.abs(t1Delay - 1000)).toBeLessThan(50);\n\n// Attempt 2: 1000 * 2^1 = 2000ms delay\ntask.availableAt = new Date(Date.now() - 10);  // Make it available\nq.getNext();\nq.fail(task.id);\nconst t2Delay = task.availableAt!.getTime() - Date.now();\nexpect(Math.abs(t2Delay - 2000)).toBeLessThan(100);\n\n// Attempt 3: 1000 * 2^2 = 4000ms delay\ntask.availableAt = new Date(Date.now() - 10);\nq.getNext();\nq.fail(task.id);\nconst t3Delay = task.availableAt!.getTime() - Date.now();\nexpect(Math.abs(t3Delay - 4000)).toBeLessThan(100);\n```\n\nSo for attempt 1:\n- `q.getNext()` → task.attempts = 1, task is now processing\n- `q.fail(task.id)` → task.attempts (1) < task.maxRetries (3)? Yes → retry\n- Delay = 1000 * 2^(1-1) = 1000 * 1 = 1000\n- availableAt is now set to Date.now() + 1000\n- t1Delay = availableAt - Date.now() = ~1000 ✓\n\nFor attempt 2:\n- `task.availableAt = new Date(Date.now() - 10)` → availableAt is now in the past\n- `q.getNext()` → finds this task in q.tasks (was put back by fail). \n  - task.availableAt ≤ Date.now() ✓ → returns it\n  - task.attempts = 2\n- `q.fail(task.id)` → task.attempts (2) < maxRetries (3)? Yes → retry\n- Delay = 1000 * 2^(2-1) = 1000 * 2 = 2000\n- availableAt = Date.now() + 2000\n- t2Delay = availableAt - Date.now() = ~2000 ✓\n\nFor attempt 3:\n- `task.availableAt = new Date(Date.now() - 10)`\n- `q.getNext()` → task.attempts = 3\n- `q.fail(task.id)` → task.attempts (3) < maxRetries (3)? 3 < 3 = false! → goes to DLQ!\n\nAH HA! The third fail sends it to DLQ because `attempts (3) < maxRetries (3)` is FALSE! So `availableAt` is NOT set for retry, and the backoff is not 4000ms.\n\nWait, but the fail code for DLQ is:\n```javascript\n} else {\n  task.status = 'failed';\n  task.error = error ?? task.error;\n  task.failedAt = new Date();\n  q.deadLetter.push({...});\n}\n```\n\nSo when going to DLQ, `task.availableAt` is NOT updated. So it's still set to `Date.now() - 10` from the test's manual assignment.\n\nThen `t3Delay = task.availableAt!.getTime() - Date.now() = (Date.now() - 10) - Date.now() = -10`. Actually, the task reference in the test is still the same object. And `availableAt` was set to `new Date(Date.now() - 10)` maybe 10ms ago. So `availableAt` might be about now minus 10ms.\n\n`t3Delay = (now - 10ms) - now = approximately -10ms`. `Math.abs(-10 - 4000) = 4010`. That matches the error!\n\nSo the issue is: with `maxRetries: 3`, the task gets 3 attempts total (attempts 1, 2, 3). On the third attempt's fail, `attempts (3) < maxRetries (3)` is false, so it goes to DLQ. The backoff for attempt 3 is never calculated.\n\nThe fix is either:\n1. Change the check to `task.attempts <= task.maxRetries` (so that maxRetries=3 means 3 retries = 4 total attempts)\n2. Or adjust the test\n\nI think the spec says \"retries\" means additional attempts after the first one. So `maxRetries: 3` means 3 retries = 4 total attempts. Let me change the check to `<=`.\n\n### Fix 2: Circular dependency tests\n\nI'll fix the tests to not expect errors for scenarios that can't produce cycles.\n\n### Fix 3: \"not currently processing\" errors\n\nThese happen because:\n- After calling `q.fail()`, the task goes to DLQ (not pending) because `attempts >= maxRetries`\n- Then the test tries to `q.getNext()` again, which doesn't find the task\n- Then the test tries `q.fail()` on a task that's not in activeTasks\n\nThis is the same root cause as Fix 1.\n\n### Fix 4: Middleware error not marking task as failed\n\nLooking at my code:\n```javascript\nconst runNext = async (): Promise<void> => {\n  // ...\n  try {\n    await fn(task, () => { nextCalled = true; });\n    if (nextCalled) {\n      await runNext();\n    }\n  } catch (err) {\n    task.error = err instanceof Error ? err : new Error(String(err));\n    this.fail(task.id, task.error);\n    return;\n  }\n};\n```\n\nThe issue is that when middleware throws, we call `this.fail(task.id, task.error)`. But `fail()` looks up the task in `q.activeTasks`. However, looking at my code more carefully - `fail()` tries to get the task from `q.activeTasks.get(taskId)`. Since the task is already in activeTasks (we called `getNext()` earlier), it should be there.\n\nWait, but `this.fail()` is an async method call inside the middleware chain. Does `this.fail()` throw because the task is not in activeTasks? Let me check.\n\nActually, the `processTask` method first checks that the task is in activeTasks:\n```javascript\nconst task = q.activeTasks.get(taskId);\nif (!task) throw new Error(`Task \"${taskId}\" is not currently processing.`);\n```\n\nThen it builds the middleware chain. When middleware throws:\n```javascript\ncatch (err) {\n  task.error = err instanceof Error ? err : new Error(String(err));\n  this.fail(task.id, task.error);\n  return;\n}\n```\n\n`this.fail(taskId, error)` will look up the task in activeTasks again: `q.activeTasks.get(taskId)`. This should find it since we haven't removed it yet.\n\nUnless...the second call to `this.fail()` accesses a different `this` context? No, it's a method call on the same object.\n\nHmm wait, let me look at the test:\n\n```javascript\nit('should mark task as failed if middleware throws', async () => {\n  q.use((_task, _next) => {\n    throw new Error('Middleware error');\n  });\n\n  q.process((_task) => {\n    // Should not reach here\n  });\n\n  const task = q.add('test');\n  q.getNext();\n  await q.processTask(task.id);\n\n  expect(task.status).toBe('failed');\n  expect(task.error?.message).toBe('Middleware error');\n});\n```\n\n`q.getNext()` moves the task to activeTasks with status 'processing'. Then `q.processTask(task.id)` is called. It finds the task in activeTasks. The middleware throws. The catch calls `this.fail(task.id, error)`.\n\nIn `fail()`:\n```javascript\nconst task = q.activeTasks.get(taskId); // Should find it!\nif (!task) throw new Error(...);\n```\n\nUnless `fail()` throws because the task HAS ALREADY BEEN FAILED?\n\nWait, the error from the test says:\n```\nAssertionError: expected 'pending' to be 'failed' // Object.is equality\n```\n\nSo `task.status` is `'pending'`, not `'failed'`. That means either `fail()` wasn't called, or it was called but the task was retried (set back to `pending`).\n\nIf `fail()` was called with attempts=1 and maxRetries=3 (default), then `attempts < maxRetries` → `1 < 3` → true → retry! The status gets set back to 'pending'.\n\nThat's the issue! When `fail()` is called from the middleware error handler, the task has `attempts=1` and `maxRetries=3`, so it's retried. The test expects it to be failed.\n\nI need to decide: should middleware errors cause immediate failure (no retry) or should they go through the normal retry cycle? The spec says: \"If a middleware throws an error, the task should be marked as failed.\"\n\n\"Marked as failed\" means the task is FAILED, not retried. So middleware errors should bypass retry and go straight to DLQ.\n\nLet me add