``\n\nSo `q.completedTaskIds.has(dep.id)` should be true because we completed `dep`. But the test fails with \"Cannot read properties of null (reading 'id')\" at `expect(q.getNext()!.id).toBe(task.id)`. That means `q.getNext()` returns null.\n\nWhy would `q.getNext()` return null after the dependent task was added with status 'pending'?\n\nThe task was added with `dependsOn: [dep.id]` and is immediately available (all deps completed). But when was the task added?\n\nWait, in the test:\n```javascript\nconst dep = q.add('dep');\nq.getNext();\nq.complete(dep.id);\n\nconst task = q.add('dependent', { dependsOn: [dep.id] });\n```\n\n`q.getNext()` gets `dep` and moves it to processing. Then `q.complete(dep.id)` marks it as completed and adds `dep.id` to `completedTaskIds`. Then `q.add('dependent', { dependsOn: [dep.id] })` creates a new task, checks that dep.id is in `completedTaskIds`, so it should be 'pending'.\n\nBut then `q.getNext()` should return this task. Unless `q.tasks` doesn't have it.\n\nHmm, let me look at the `add` code more carefully:\n\n```javascript\nadd(payload, options) {\n  // ...\n  const q = this.getOrCreateQueue(queueName);\n  q.tasks.push(task);\n  // ...\n}\n```\n\nAfter `add`, the task is pushed to `q.tasks`. Then `getNext()` should find it.\n\nWait, could the issue be that `getNext()` returns null because there's some filtering issue? Let me trace through `getNext()`:\n\n1. `q = this.getQueue(name)` → returns the queue\n2. Check concurrency: `q.concurrencyLimit !== null && q.activeTasks.size >= q.concurrencyLimit` → concurrency is null (not set), so this is false\n3. `const now = new Date()`\n4. Filter candidates: `q.tasks.filter(t => t.status === 'pending' && t.dependsOn.length === 0 && (!t.availableAt || t.availableAt <= now))`\n\nThe new task has `dependsOn: [dep.id]`. Wait! The check is `t.dependsOn.length === 0`. The new task has `dependsOn: [dep.id]` (length 1), so it's FILTERED OUT!\n\nThat's the bug! In `getNext()`, I'm checking `t.dependsOn.length === 0` but the task's dependencies have ALL been completed! The task should be pending and available, but it still has `dependsOn` entries.\n\nThe issue is that my `getNext()` filters out ALL tasks with `dependsOn` entries, regardless of whether they're completed. I should instead only filter out tasks whose dependencies are NOT all completed.\n\nWhen adding the task, if all deps are already completed, I set `task.status = 'pending'`. But the task still has `dependsOn.length > 0`. So `getNext()` filters it out.\n\nThe fix is to change the filter in `getNext()` from `t.dependsOn.length === 0` to checking if any unresolved dependencies exist:\n\n```javascript\nconst candidates = q.tasks.filter((t) => {\n  if (t.status !== 'pending') return false;\n  if (t.availableAt && t.availableAt > now) return false;\n  // Check if all dependencies are resolved\n  if (t.dependsOn.length > 0) {\n    const allResolved = t.dependsOn.every(depId => q.completedTaskIds.has(depId));\n    if (!allResolved) return false;\n  }\n  return true;\n});\n```\n\nThis is more correct. When a task's dependencies are all completed, the task is effectively \"pending\" even though it still has `dependsOn` entries.\n\nSimilarly, in the `getWaitingCount()` method, I need to fix the same filter.\n\nAnd in `checkDependents()`, when a dependency completes, it sets the task status to 'pending'. But the task still has `dependsOn` entries. So it would be filtered out by `getNext()`.\n\nThe core issue is that I keep `dependsOn` as the original list, but I need a way to know which dependencies are resolved. I either need to:\n1. Clear `dependsOn` when all deps are resolved\n2. Or check against `completedTaskIds` in the filter\n\nOption 2 is cleaner because it preserves the original dependency list for inspection.\n\nLet me update both `getNext()` and `getWaitingCount()` to use this approach.\n\nAlso for the `checkDependents()` method - when all deps are resolved, it sets status to 'pending'. But the task should now be findable by `getNext()`. With option 2, this works correctly because `getNext()` checks `completedTaskIds`.\n\nLet me make these fixes now.\n\nOne more issue with the dependency test \"should unblock tasks when their dependencies complete\":\n```javascript\nconst dep = q.add('dependency');\nconst task = q.add('dependent', { dependsOn: [dep.id] });\n\n// Process and complete the dependency\nq.getNext();\nq.complete(dep.id);\n\n// Dependent should now be unblocked\nexpect(task.status).toBe('pending');\nconst retrieved = q.getNext();\nexpect(retrieved).not.toBeNull();\nexpect(retrieved!.id).toBe(task.id);\n```\n\nIn `checkDependents`, when dep completes, it checks:\n```javascript\nconst allDone = task.dependsOn.every((dId) => q.completedTaskIds.has(dId));\nif (allDone) {\n  task.status = 'pending';\n}\n```\n\nSo `task.status` becomes 'pending'. Then `getNext()` finds it (now pending, and dependencies are all resolved in the filter check). This should work with the fix.\n\nOK, let me now fix all the issues:\n\n1. Change `fail()` to use `attempts <= maxRetries` \n2. Fix `getNext()` filter to check completedTaskIds instead of `dependsOn.length === 0`\n3. Fix `getWaitingCount()` similarly\n4. Make middleware errors fail the task permanently (not retry)\n5. Fix the circular dependency tests\n6. Fix the backoff test tolerance\n7. Fix concurrency active count test\n\nLet me do all these fixes now.","signature":"80fb643a-c1ac-4a79-9e0f-a38f28280fb9"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3507,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":66688,"output_tokens":0,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2dc7e0af-5fc2-4b88-b1fc-844f6d81dc8e","uuid":"c94d9368-8c21-48d7-91c9-0ef3f3691aa1"}
